data.content:
Here are the improved MSTest assertions for your collection tests:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));  // this one is fine
    var admin = users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin");
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);
}
```

**Key replacements:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsTrue(x.Any())` or `Assert.AreEqual(expected, x.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, x.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(collection.All(...))` | Already fine — no built-in equivalent |

**Why?** Specific assertions produce better failure messages. `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` tells you "Expected: 3, Actual: 1", while `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)` just says "Expected: True, Actual: False".

If you're on **MSTest v3+**, you also get `CollectionAssert` methods:

```csharp
CollectionAssert.AllSatisfy(users, u => Assert.IsTrue(u.IsActive));
```
